{
  "esm": "1.1.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "MadronichJValues",
    "description": "Stage 5 of WRF-Chem's Madronich photolysis (phot_opt = 1): the wavelength integral that turns an actinic flux into the 21 photolysis frequencies the RADM2 mechanism reads (chem/module_phot_mad.F:1955-1970 and :1604-1624, WRF v4.8.0).\n\nj = sum over bins of fext(kl) (fldir + fldn + flup) sigma(kl) phi(kl), over the 101 active bins kl0..kl1 = 30..130. The static spectra are the scheme's own xs/xqy tables, transcribed; the eleven temperature- and pressure-dependent ones come from the mounted MadronichSpectra subsystem.\n\nUNIT CHAIN, which is worth stating because it is the one place an implementation can be right and still be wrong by a factor of 3600: this sum is photolysis_mad's d(nr,lev), in s^-1. madronich1_driver multiplies it by 60 into ph_* (min^-1), and WRF's generated KPP interface divides by 60 again (`jv(Pj_x) = ph_x(i,k,j)/60.`). So the numbers here ARE jv(1:21) of the integrator. That identity was checked against the independent RADM2 dumps at four model times and holds to 1.1e-7 relative, the real32 round trip.\n\nWHAT IS OPEN: the three actinic-flux components are PARAMETERS here, not equations. They are the output of SUBROUTINE optics and its delta-Eddington two-stream solver ps2str, which is not yet written -- see PLAN/INVENTORY. Supplying them from the instrumented Fortran lets this stage be pinned on its own, and it is: the 21 j-values match WRF at five levels spanning the model column. Everything from the column state to the j-values is therefore verified EXCEPT the radiative transfer itself.\n\nA CORRECTION to this project's earlier diagnosis, from measuring the dumped tables rather than reading the comments. WRF's `ch3coo2h 20 actually use 0.28*(h2o2 value)` is a note on how the xs(:,20) column was BUILT, not a relation the code evaluates: the actual ratio xs(:,20)/xs(:,10) over the 60 bins where both are non-zero runs from 0.1414 to 0.2874 and is not constant. It is a stored table and is transcribed as one. The same goes for the `estimate, no reliable measurement` spectrum of HCO-CH=CH-CHO. The substantive point is unchanged: an implementation that builds these spectra from the literature instead of from these tables will not reproduce WRF, because no measured peroxyacetic-acid spectrum is 0.276 times hydrogen peroxide's.\n\nALSO MEASURED, and it removes a whole subroutine from the work: the O2 Schumann-Runge parameterization (SUBROUTINE srband, the sra/srb tables, and the cvo2 column that feeds it) is DEAD CODE in the shipped configuration. srband's first statement inside its wavelength loop is `IF (wl(kl)>205.) RETURN`, and the loop starts at kl0 = 30 where wl = 254.8 nm, so it returns before doing anything; xs(kl,1), the O2 cross section, is exactly zero in all 101 active bins; d(1,lev) -- the O2 photolysis rate -- is identically zero; and phot1(n) = d(n+1) drops that row regardless. No Schumann-Runge component is written here because there is nothing for it to contribute. Reactivating it would mean setting kl0 = 1, which WRF's own comment at :1722 contemplates."
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NBIN": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 101,
      "description": "Number of active wavelength bins, kl1-kl0+1 = 101."
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "wlbin": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NBIN"
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "MadronichJValues": {
      "subsystems": {
        "sp": {
          "ref": "./spectra.esm"
        }
      },
      "variables": {
        "flux_direct": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "shape": [
            "wlbin"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "Normalized direct-beam actinic flux at this level, fldir(lev,kl) as SUBROUTINE optics/ps2str returns it. THIS IS THE COMPONENT'S OPEN EDGE: it is supplied here, not computed, because the delta-Eddington two-stream solve that produces it is not yet written (see the description)."
        },
        "flux_down": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "shape": [
            "wlbin"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "Normalized down-welling diffuse actinic flux, fldn(lev,kl)."
        },
        "flux_up": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "shape": [
            "wlbin"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "Normalized up-welling diffuse actinic flux, flup(lev,kl)."
        },
        "actinic": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "wlbin"
          ],
          "units": "cm^-2 s^-1",
          "description": "Actinic flux per bin, df = fext(kl) (fldir + fldn + flup) (module_phot_mad.F:1959). fext is the extraterrestrial irradiance in photons per bin, so df is already a photon flux and each j-value is a bare sum of df sigma phi over bins."
        },
        "j_o31d": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "O3 -> O(1D). Both factors are recomputed from the local temperature. photolysis_mad's d(2,lev), exported as phot1(1,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(1) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_o33p": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "O3 -> O(3P). Same spectrum as the O(1D) channel; the yields split them. photolysis_mad's d(3,lev), exported as phot1(2,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(2) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_no2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "NO2 -> NO + O(3P). RADM2 reaction J01, the mechanism's single most important photolysis rate. photolysis_mad's d(4,lev), exported as phot1(3,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(3) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_no3o2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "NO3 -> NO + O2 (RADM2 J07). photolysis_mad's d(5,lev), exported as phot1(4,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(4) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_no3o": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "NO3 -> NO2 + O(3P) (RADM2 J08). photolysis_mad's d(6,lev), exported as phot1(5,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(5) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_hno2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "HONO -> OH + NO (RADM2 J04). photolysis_mad's d(7,lev), exported as phot1(6,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(6) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_hno3": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "HNO3 -> OH + NO2 (RADM2 J05). photolysis_mad's d(8,lev), exported as phot1(7,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(7) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_hno4": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "HNO4 (RADM2 J06). photolysis_mad's d(9,lev), exported as phot1(8,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(8) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_h2o2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "H2O2 -> 2 OH (RADM2 J09). photolysis_mad's d(10,lev), exported as phot1(9,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(9) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_ch2or": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "HCHO -> HO2 + HO2 + CO, the radical channel (RADM2 J11). photolysis_mad's d(11,lev), exported as phot1(10,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(10) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_ch2om": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "HCHO -> CO + H2, the molecular channel (RADM2 J10); pressure-quenched above 330 nm. photolysis_mad's d(12,lev), exported as phot1(11,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(11) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_ch3cho": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "ALD -> MO2 + HO2 + CO (RADM2 J12); Stern-Volmer quenched. photolysis_mad's d(13,lev), exported as phot1(12,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(12) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_ch3coch3": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "Acetone. RADM2 uses it for OP2, its higher organic peroxide (J14). photolysis_mad's d(14,lev), exported as phot1(13,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(13) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_ch3coc2h5": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "Methyl ethyl ketone; RADM2's KET (J16). The yield is acetone's, copied. photolysis_mad's d(15,lev), exported as phot1(14,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(14) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_hcocho": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "Glyoxal channel a (RADM2 J17); Stern-Volmer quenched. photolysis_mad's d(16,lev), exported as phot1(15,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(15) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_ch3cocho": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "Methylglyoxal (RADM2 J19); Stern-Volmer quenched. photolysis_mad's d(17,lev), exported as phot1(16,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(16) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_hcochest": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "DCB, HCO-CH=CH-CHO (RADM2 J20). Its 64-bin spectrum is the one WRF labels `estimate, no reliable measurement`. photolysis_mad's d(18,lev), exported as phot1(17,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(17) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_ch3o2h": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "OP1, methyl hydroperoxide (RADM2 J13). photolysis_mad's d(19,lev), exported as phot1(18,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(18) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_ch3coo2h": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "PAA, peroxyacetic acid (RADM2 J15). Its spectrum is the one WRF labels `actually use 0.28*(h2o2 value)`; see the component description -- it is a stored table, not a runtime relation. photolysis_mad's d(20,lev), exported as phot1(19,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(19) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_ch3ono2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "ONIT, organic nitrate (RADM2 J21). photolysis_mad's d(21,lev), exported as phot1(20,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(20) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        },
        "j_hcochob": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "description": "Glyoxal channel b (RADM2 J18); Stern-Volmer quenched. photolysis_mad's d(22,lev), exported as phot1(21,lev); WRF stores 60 times it in ph_* and the KPP interface divides by 60 again, so this IS jv(21) of the RADM2 integrator, in s^-1."
        }
      },
